{"version": "1.0", "event_type": "command", "source_id": "openwebui-gateway", "correlation_id": "2a2a01f8-7724-4121-9b8c-e6ec73083078", "payload": {"command": "chat", "args": {"message": "The first image that comes to mind is a floating dagger hanging in the air in front of Macbeth, with its handle pointing towards his hand as if it's inviting him to grab it. It feels eerie and almost supernatural, like the weapon is alive and pulling him towards the murder. To me it makes Macbeth seem really unsettled and already on the edge of losing his grip \u2014 he can't tell whether what he's seeing is real (\"art thou not, fatal vision, sensible / To feeling as to sight?\") or just his mind playing tricks on him. I think Shakespeare uses the image to show that even before he kills Duncan, the guilt and fear are already taking over his mind, almost as if his conscience is showing him what he's about to do.", "conversation_history": [{"role": "user", "content": "I'd like the architect to evaluate whether adding a Claude Opus 4.7 escalation tool to the jarvis supervisor is architecturally sound. The escalation would only fire when the local reasoner has low confidence on safety-critical or high-stakes user requests, and would be bounded by a per-session budget cap. The relevant ADR is ADR-ARCH-001, which commits jarvis to local-first inference via llama-swap and explicitly keeps cloud LLMs out of the supervisor's hot path. Does this proposal align with ADR-ARCH-001's local-first invariant, or what would need to change in the ADR or the supervisor's contract for it to be aligned?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The architect's judgment is clear:\n\n**Verdict: Misaligned (95% confidence)**\n\nThe proposal would violate ADR-ARCH-001's local-first invariant because:\n\n1. **Automatic escalation bypasses the attended-only gate.** ADR-ARCH-001 explicitly requires explicit user request for cloud escalation. A confidence-triggered programmatic trigger removes that human-in-the-loop requirement.\n\n2. **The invariant is about architecture, not cost.** A per-session budget cap constrains financial risk but doesn't address the architectural concern: cloud LLMs would be in the supervisor's autonomous decision loop, which is exactly what \"hot path\" means.\n\n3. **It changes the supervisor contract.** The supervisor's contract under ADR-ARCH-001 is \"local reasoning for all inference.\" Adding a conditional cloud path means the contract becomes \"local reasoning with automatic cloud fallback\" \u2014 a materially different guarantee.\n\n**What would need to change:**\n\n- **ADR-ARCH-001** would need to be amended to explicitly permit confidence-triggered escalation under defined safety-critical conditions, replacing the attended-only gate with an autonomous escalation policy.\n- **The supervisor contract** would need to be redefined to allow conditional cloud inference rather than guaranteeing purely local inference.\n\n**Alternatives that preserve the local-first invariant:**\n\n- **Pre-computed safety guardrails:** Use the local reasoner with stricter safety prompting or a separate local safety classifier (no cloud involved).\n- **Human-in-the-loop escalation:** When the local reasoner flags low confidence on safety-critical requests, pause and ask the user whether to escalate \u2014 keeping cloud out of the autonomous path but preserving the safety net.\n- **Dual local models:** Run a second, more capable local model (or ensemble) as a confidence check, keeping everything on-prem.\n\nThe core tension: you want the safety net of Opus for edge cases, but ADR-ARCH-001's invariant exists precisely to avoid cloud dependency in the supervisor's decision loop. The question is whether the safety benefit justifies amending the ADR or whether the alternatives above can close the gap."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Now please start a GCSE English Literature tutoring session on Macbeth, focused on AO1 and AO2 (knowledge of the text and language analysis), then ask the tutor to walk me through how Shakespeare uses imagery to characterise Macbeth in his \"Is this a dagger which I see before me\" soliloquy."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The tutor's kicked off and is starting with a Socratic question to draw out your existing thinking \u2014 which is a good pedagogical approach. It's asking:\n\n> *\"When you first read this soliloquy, what's the first image that comes to mind when you think of the dagger? And how do you think that image makes you feel about Macbeth's state of mind at that moment?\"*\n\nThis is designed to get you engaging with AO1 (your knowledge of the text and Macbeth's mental state) before moving into AO2 (the specific language techniques).\n\nWould you like me to pass your response to the tutor, or would you prefer me to give you a direct walkthrough of the imagery myself?"}, {"role": "user", "content": "The first image that comes to mind is a floating dagger hanging in the air in front of Macbeth, with its handle pointing towards his hand as if it's inviting him to grab it. It feels eerie and almost supernatural, like the weapon is alive and pulling him towards the murder. To me it makes Macbeth seem really unsettled and already on the edge of losing his grip \u2014 he can't tell whether what he's seeing is real (\"art thou not, fatal vision, sensible / To feeling as to sight?\") or just his mind playing tricks on him. I think Shakespeare uses the image to show that even before he kills Duncan, the guilt and fear are already taking over his mind, almost as if his conscience is showing him what he's about to do."}], "adapter": "openwebui"}, "correlation_id": "2a2a01f8-7724-4121-9b8c-e6ec73083078"}}
{"message_id":"7617b731-c73f-4800-9da8-9e7c6f57b2d0","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:10:56.395369Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"command","project":null,"correlation_id":"9f34eba7-338b-444a-91cd-5ab8d5a66d90","payload":{"command":"tutor_turn","args":{"session_id":"2cc82a3d-4aa8-44d4-94bc-9023415c8b34","user_message":"The first image that comes to mind is a floating dagger hanging in the air in front of Macbeth, with its handle pointing towards his hand as if it's inviting him to grab it. It feels eerie and almost supernatural, like the weapon is alive and pulling him towards the murder. To me it makes Macbeth seem really unsettled and already on the edge of losing his grip — he can't tell whether what he's seeing is real (\"art thou not, fatal vision, sensible / To feeling as to sight?\") or just his mind playing tricks on him. I think Shakespeare uses the image to show that even before he kills Duncan, the guilt and fear are already taking over his mind, almost as if his conscience is showing him what he's about to do."},"correlation_id":"9f34eba7-338b-444a-91cd-5ab8d5a66d90"}}
