h is alone in his chamber. The ghost appears at Act 3 Scene 4 in front of his entire court. The tutor is asking: what does it mean that his breakdown is now *public*? How does this affect how we read his mental state and his position as a leader?\n\n2. **Shift in language focus** \u2014 From the *weapon* (\"dudgeon-griped\" dagger) to the *evidence* (\"gory locks\"). The tutor wants you to think about how the language itself tracks Macbeth's psychological shift from planning violence to confronting its consequences.\n\n**Where this is heading:**\nThe tutor is scaffolding you towards a Grade 9-level analysis that weaves together:\n- Comparative imagery analysis (AO2)\n- Character development across the play (AO1)\n- Structural significance of scene placement (AO4)\n- Language detail and word-level analysis (AO2)\n\n**Their next question for you:**\n> *What other language choices in the ghost scene reinforce this sense of loss of control?*\n\nThis is a chance to dig into more specific language \u2014 think about imperatives (\"never shake\"), the physicality of the description, or how Macbeth's speech patterns break down compared to the dagger soliloquy. Want to have a go, or shall I suggest pushing the tutor in a different direction?\nUSER: I think the imperatives really stand out. When Macbeth shouts \"Avaunt! and quit my sight! let the earth hide thee!\" he's giving three commands in a row, almost like he's panicking and trying to control something that obviously won't obey him. That's the opposite of the dagger soliloquy where his speech was more measured \u2014 there he asks questions and reasons with himself, but here his lines are short and full of exclamation marks. He also fixates on the ghost's body \u2014 \"Thy bones are marrowless, thy blood is cold\" \u2014 as if he's trying to argue it out of existence by listing physical reasons it shouldn't be there. Compared to the dagger, where Macbeth doubted his own senses, here he's lost any sense of control over 